{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_ubyzpntjbpep6gqbgwbqx7hzmwjj77oyjpq72ack4qzdvxwnwrba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 6 tests pass:\n- `test_rect_pack_fail_paging`\n- `test_rect_pack_no_paging`\n- `test_rect_pack_one_rect_per_page_paging`\n- `test_rect_pack_fill`\n- `test_rect_pack_uniform_paging`\n- `test_rect_pack_loop`\n\nThe C-to-Rust translation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did:\n\n1. **`Rect::new`** — Constructs a new rectangle with default `RectOutInfo`.\n\n2. **`RectPacker::pack`** — The main entry point. Sorts rectangles by max-side descending (with min-side as a tiebreaker, matching the C `compare_rect_r_max_side` logic), resets all info, then iterates pages until done.\n\n3. **`BinNode::find`** / `find_mut` — Recursively searches the bin tree for a free node large enough; mirrors C's `find_bin_tree`.\n\n4. **`BinNode::split`** — Marks node as used and creates `down`/`right` children, matching `split_bin_tree`.\n\n5. **`BinNode::grow_right` / `grow_down`** — Expand the root, replacing it and its children, mirroring the C versions which \"save\" the old root via a copy.\n\n6. **`BinNode::grow`** — Picks growth direction based on the same heuristic as `grow_bin_tree`.\n\n7. **`pack_bin_tree` (helper)** — Mirrors the C function: tries to find or grow space for each unpacked rectangle, tracks `all_fit`/`none_fit`, and updates `next`/`last` for the next page.\n\nThe implementation is fully safe Rust (no `unsafe`), uses idiomatic `Option<Box<…>>` for tree edges, and avoids FFI per the rules."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":849,"cache_read_input_tokens":37316,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":849,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fda3da7-a7f3-4c34-b2b9-0d449f6bc375",